.TH ICONV 1
.SH NAME
iconv \- convert an image's pixel-channel format, in Limbo
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B iconv
[
.B -u
]
[
.BI -c\  chanstr
]
[
.I file
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Iconv
is a structural Limbo translation of Plan 9's own
.IR iconv (1)
.RB ( sys/src/cmd/iconv.c ,
102 lines in the 9front tree) \(em an image pixel-channel converter,
not the better-known Unix text-encoding
.IR iconv .
See
.B doc/plan9-port.md
in the source tree for the translation-not-workalike distinction. It
lives under
.B p9/cmd
rather than
.BR appl/cmd ;
see
.B p9/dis/README
for how to bind it in.
.PP
.I Iconv
reads an image (standard input, or
.I file
if given), converts it to a channel format, and writes the result to
standard output. With no
.BR -c ,
the output channel format is the same as the input's \(em still a real
conversion pass, not a bare copy, since it goes through image
allocation and drawing exactly as
.B -c
does.
.SS Options
.TP
.BI -c\  chanstr
Convert to the channel format described by
.IR chanstr
(e.g.
.BR r8g8b8 ,
.BR k8 ,
\&...). A malformed descriptor is an error.
.TP
.B -u
Write the result in an uncompressed, easy-to-parse format instead of
the normal (possibly compressed) image file format: a single text
header line \(em the channel descriptor and the image's four rectangle
coordinates, each right-justified in an 11-character field and
separated by a space, with a trailing space \(em followed immediately
by the raw pixel bytes, one scanline at a time, with no further
framing.
.SH SOURCE
.B /p9/cmd/iconv.b
(translated from
.BR sys/src/cmd/iconv.c )
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR iconvtest (1)
.SH BUGS
Unlike every other translation in
.BR p9/cmd ,
this one does not port any image-codec logic: this tree's own
.B Draw
module (see
.BR draw (2))
already wraps Plan 9's real
.BR libdraw / libmemdraw
C functions directly for channel-descriptor parsing, image allocation,
compositing, and the standard image file format, so
.I iconv
here is mostly wiring those together in the same order the original
C does. Only
.BR -u 's
hand-rolled output format (which has no
.B Draw
equivalent) and the
.B bytesperline
calculation it needs (translated directly from this tree's own
.B libdraw/bytesperline.c )
are actually new code.
.PP
Because the conversion itself is pre-existing, already-shipped Inferno
functionality rather than something this translation implements,
.IR iconvtest (1)
checks the real conversions it exercises only by shape (channel
descriptor, depth, rectangle) rather than exact pixel values \(em see
its own description for why.
